* text=auto eol=lf

# Frozen third-party regression fixtures: byte-exact or worthless. A .ppm has an
# ASCII header and may contain no NUL at all, so git's content sniffing calls it
# text and text=auto would rewrite CRLF in its payload on commit. Say binary
# outright rather than relying on the sniff.
test/fixtures/jpeg/*.jpg binary
test/fixtures/jpeg/*.ppm binary

# Incremental-save outputs qpdf approved: byte-exact or worthless, since the
# whole point is that bytes an external implementation vouched for cannot drift.
# Measured, and the sniff does NOT save these: three of the four contain no NUL
# at all, so git calls them text, and `classic-onto-xref-stream.pdf` carries a
# lone CR inside its deflate stream that eol=lf would rewrite -- it escapes
# today only because one NUL happens to land in the sniff window. Say binary.
test/fixtures/qpdf/*.pdf binary

# The WPT tree-construction corpus carries lone CR bytes inside #data payloads
# (processing-instructions.dat's `<?hey\rthere=1?>` is one) and other bytes the
# format deliberately makes unsafe. eol=lf normalization leaves a lone CR alone
# today, but these are byte-exact-or-worthless like the jpeg fixtures above, so
# say so rather than relying on that. -text rather than binary: they are a text
# corpus people read in a diff.
test/fixtures/wpt/tree-construction/*.dat -text
